\begin_layout Section
Governing Problem Statement:
 Total Field Computation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Calculating The Total Field (
\begin_inset Formula $\vec{E}_{total}=\vec{E}_{ext}+\vec{E}_{sp}+\vec{E}_{I_{rod}}+\vec{E}_{I_{plane}}$
\end_inset

)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The primary objective of this simulation is to compute the total,
 instantaneous electric field distribution 
\begin_inset Formula $\vec{E}_{total}$
\end_inset

 within a highly divergent rod-plane gap during a corona discharge event.
 The presence of 
\bar under
drifting space charge
\bar default
 fundamentally alters the geometric field,
 requiring a coupled 
\series bold
electrostatic solution
\series default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace defskip
\end_inset

To determine the net electric field at any point in space,
 we employ the principle of linear superposition,
 modeling the total field as a vector sum of four distinct components:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\vec{E}_{total}=\vec{E}_{ext}+\vec{E}_{sp}+\vec{E}_{I_{rod}}+\vec{E}_{I_{plane}}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\bar under
where
\bar default
:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $\vec{E}_{ext}$
\end_inset

:
 Is the external (geometric) electric field caused solely by the applied voltage on the electrodes in a completely uncharged medium.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $\vec{E}_{sp}$
\end_inset

 Is the space charge electric field generated directly by the cloud of drifting ions in the inter-electrode gap.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $\vec{E}_{I_{rod}}$
\end_inset

:
 Is the image charge field accounting for the boundary conditions on the curved surface of the rod-electrode (ensuring the total potential on the conductor matches the boundary values).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $\vec{E}_{I_{plane}}$
\end_inset

:
 Is the image charge field enforcing the zero-potential boundary condition on the grounded plane-electrode.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Foundational Simplifications and Physical Assumptions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To maintain computational feasibility while preserving physical accuracy,
 the numerical model operates under three core physical simplifications regarding the dielectric medium (air):
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Electrostatic Linearity
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quote
By expressing 
\begin_inset Formula $\vec{E}_{total}$
\end_inset

 as a straightforward algebraic sum of its components,
 we assume the governing medium behaves linearly.
 This implies that the presence of space charge does not alter the fundamental permittivity of the medium,
 allowing individual fields to exist independently and sum together without cross-modulation or non-linear coupling effects.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Constant Relative Permittivity
\series default
 (
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon_{r}$
\end_inset

)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quote
We assume that the relative permittivity of the air medium is perfectly constant and uniform throughout the entire gap (
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon_{r}\approx1$
\end_inset

).
 In a real corona discharge,
 the intense localized electric field creates a highly thermalized ionization zone (plasma sheath) where localized temperature spikes,
 gas density drops,
 and electron-ion densities could technically alter the local dielectric constant.
 For this kinematics model,
 these micro-scale fluctuations are assumed to have a negligible impact on the macro-scale field distribution.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Isotropic and Homogeneous Medium
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quote
The medium is modeled as isotropic and homogeneous.
 This means its physical properties,
 specifically its permittivity and ion mobility are identical in all directions and at all spatial coordinates.
 The electric displacement vector 
\begin_inset Formula $\vec{D}$
\end_inset

 remains perfectly parallel to the electric field vector 
\begin_inset Formula $\vec{E}$
\end_inset

 via:
 
\begin_inset Formula $\vec{D}=\epsilon_{r}\epsilon_{0}\vec{E}$
\end_inset

.
 This assumption ensures that the field’s direction depends purely on the spatial layout of the charges and electrodes,
 rather than any directional preferences or crystal-like biases in the gas medium itself.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Newpage pagebreak
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Theoritical Analysis
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Space Charge Density Function (
\begin_inset Formula $\rho(r)$
\end_inset

)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In order to calculate the 
\series bold
Total Electric Field distribution
\series default
 due to 
\bar under
Space Charge
\bar default
 (and its images),
 we first need a function that describes the 
\bar under
space charge density as a function of space
\bar default
.
 Since this Thesis is based on the work of 
\series bold
Wang Wenduan,
 Wang Haiting 
\series default

\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

A New Model For Simulating The Effects Of Space Charge In Gas Discharge
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset


\series bold
 [1]
\series default
,
 we will use their improved model,
 that is given by:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\rho(r)=\frac{A}{\left(r+C\right)^{B}}\;\;\left[\frac{C}{m^{3}}\right]\label{eq:space_charge_density}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\bar under
where
\bar default
:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $A[C\cdot m^{B-3}]$
\end_inset

:
 
\series bold
Peak Charge Density / Scaling Amplitude
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quote
This coefficient governs the overall intensity or scale of the space charge distribution.
 Physically,
 when evaluating the system at the absolute boundary or origin line (
\begin_inset Formula $r\to0$
\end_inset

),
 
\series bold
A
\series default
 acts as the baseline factor that determines the maximum localized charge density in tandem with 
\series bold
C
\series default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $B[\text{Dimensionless}]$
\end_inset

:
 
\series bold
Spatial Decay Exponent / Attenuation Rate
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quote
This parameter dictates how aggressively the space charge density drops off as you move radially outward from the discharge source.
 A higher value of 
\series bold
B
\series default
 represents a highly localized,
 rapidly decaying plasma sheath or ionization zone,
 whereas a lower value indicates a more dispersed,
 long-range space charge cloud.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $C\;[m]$
\end_inset

:
 
\series bold
Spatial Core Radius / Boundary Singularity Regulator
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quote
This is a spatial offset parameter that prevents physical and mathematical singularities at the inner boundary.
 By ensuring that 
\begin_inset Formula $lim_{r\to0}\rho(r)=\frac{A}{C^{B}}$
\end_inset

,
 it models the finite physical limits of the electrode or the core of the discharge zone,
 keeping the initial charge density stable and finite instead of letting it approach infinity 
\series bold
[1]
\series default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $r\;[m]$
\end_inset

:
 
\series bold
Radial Distance / Kinematic Coordinate
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quote
The distance measured radially outward from the apex (tip) of the rod-electrode into the inter-electrode gap.
 It serves as the primary spatial coordinate for tracking the kinematics and localized attenuation of the drifting ions or space charge.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
A Note On The Geometric Field (
\begin_inset Formula $\vec{E}_{ext}$
\end_inset

) & The Plane Image Field (
\begin_inset Formula $\vec{E}_{I_{plane}}$
\end_inset

)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In this chapter,
 we are focused on the calculation of the 
\bar under
total electric field distribution
\bar default
 due to 
\series bold
space charge
\series default
,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\vec{E}_{sp,total}=\vec{E}_{sp}+\vec{E}_{I_{rod}}+\vec{E}_{I_{plane}}$
\end_inset

.
 The calculation of the external (applied) geometric field 
\begin_inset Formula $\vec{E}_{ext}$
\end_inset

,
 will be described later using the 
\series bold
Charge Simulation Method [2]
\series default
.
 Also,
 its important to mention that this Thesis is based on the work of 
\series bold
Wang Wenduan,
 Wang Haiting 
\series default

\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

A New Model For Simulating The Effects Of Space Charge In Gas Discharge
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset


\series bold
 [1]
\series default
,
 where they assume 
\begin_inset Formula $\vec{E}_{I_{plane}}\approx0$
\end_inset

,
 since they study the space charge effects 
\series bold
only on the region close to the rod tip
\series default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Newpage pagebreak
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Calculating The Space Charge Electric Field (
\begin_inset Formula $\vec{E}_{sp}$
\end_inset

)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand formatted
reference "fig:vector_schematic_for_the_spherical_rod_tip_and_space_charge_cone"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

,
 the minimum required geometric configuration for calculating the electric field due to space charge is illustrated.
 The rod electrode is modeled as a sphere,
 centered at the origin of the global reference frame (
\begin_inset Formula $\vec{O}=0\hat{x}+0\hat{y}+0\hat{z}$
\end_inset

),
 while the space charge envelope is enclosed within a conical geometry.
 The apex (tip) of this cone is positioned at 
\begin_inset Formula $\vec{P}_{apex}=R\hat{z}$
\end_inset

 (where 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

 is the radius of the spherical rod tip),
 and it extends along the central axis of symmetry to a maximum length of 
\begin_inset Formula $\Vert\vec{P}_{cs}\Vert=L_{cs}$
\end_inset

 (where 
\begin_inset Formula $cs$
\end_inset

 denotes the 
\series bold
C
\series default
orona 
\series bold
S
\series default
treamer boundary).
\end_layout

