\begin_layout Section
Mathematical Equivalence and Reduction to the Wang–Haiting Formulation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To validate the analytical formulation that was developed,
 it is instructive to demonstrate its exact mathematical equivalence to the foundational space-charge model published by Wang and Haiting 
\series bold
[1]
\series default
 (1988).
 While the Wang–Haiting equations rely on intermediate angular definitions (
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{3},\theta_{4}$
\end_inset

) and local trigonometric projections,
 our implementation utilizes coordinate-based algebraic forms designed for computational integration efficiency.
 This section provides a complete step-by-step proof mapping the field expressions derived in this work to the classic Wang–Haiting equations,
 accounting for coordinate origin shifts,
 corrected typographical errors in the original literature,
 and physical approximations.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Coordinate System Alignment & Translation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A primary distinction between the two models lies in the choice of global coordinate origin:
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Present Model Origin (
\begin_inset Formula $\vec{O}$
\end_inset

):

\series default
 Placed at the 
\series bold
center of the hemispherical rod
\series default
 while the apex of the cone sits at 
\begin_inset Formula $(0,0,R)$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate

\series bold
Wang–Haiting Origin (
\begin_inset Formula $\vec{O}_{\text{WH}}$
\end_inset

):

\series default
 Placed directly at the 
\series bold
apex of the 
\series default
cone (rod tip).
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $Z_{\text{WH}}$
\end_inset

 represent the axial coordinate measured along the symmetry axis in the Wang–Haiting system.
 The spatial transformation between the observation coordinate 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

 in our coordinate frame and 
\begin_inset Formula $Z_{\text{WH}}$
\end_inset

 is given by:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
Z_{\text{WH}}=z-R\implies z=Z_{\text{WH}}+R
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Reduction of the Direct Space-Charge Field (
\begin_inset Formula $E_{sp}$
\end_inset

)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
From 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:final_Esp"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

,
 the direct space-charge field component along the axis of symmetry 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}$
\end_inset

 is expressed as:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\vec{E}_{sp}(0,0,z)=\frac{1}{2\epsilon_{r}\epsilon_{0}}\int^{\theta_{B}/2}_{0}\int^{L_{cs}}_{0}\frac{\rho(r)\,r^{2}\sin(\theta)}{r^{3}_{1}}(z-R-r\cos(\theta))\,dr\,d\theta\,\hat{z}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{1}$
\end_inset

 is the algebraic scalar distance from a charge element 
\begin_inset Formula $\vec{P}_{dq_{sp}}$
\end_inset

 to the observation point 
\begin_inset Formula $(0,0,z)$
\end_inset

:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
r_{1}=\sqrt{\left[z-R-r\cos(\theta)\right]^{2}+\left[r\sin(\theta)\right]^{2}}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Step 1:
 Origin Translation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Quote
Substitute the transformation 
\begin_inset Formula $z=Z_{\text{WH}}+R$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
r_{1}=\sqrt{\left[Z_{\text{WH}}-r\cos(\theta)\right]^{2}+\left[r\sin(\theta)\right]^{2}}=\sqrt{\left[r\cos(\theta)-Z_{\text{WH}}\right]^{2}+\left[r\sin(\theta)\right]^{2}}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Quote
and for the field equation:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\vec{E}_{sp}=-\frac{1}{2\epsilon_{r}\epsilon_{0}}\int^{\theta_{B}/2}_{0}\int^{L_{cs}}_{0}\frac{\rho(r)\,r^{2}\sin(\theta)}{r^{3}_{1}}(r\cos(\theta)-Z_{\text{WH}})\,dr\,d\theta\,\hat{z}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Step 2:
 Verification with Wang–Haiting Equation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In Wang and Haiting (1988),
 for the space-charge field is given as:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E_{sp}=-\frac{1}{2\epsilon}\int^{\theta_{B}/2}_{0}\int^{L_{cs}}_{0}\frac{\rho(r)\,r^{2}\sin(\theta)}{r^{3}_{1}}(r\cos(\theta)-Z)\,dr\,d\theta
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Newpage pagebreak
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
with distance kernel:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
r_{1}=\sqrt{(r\cos(\theta)-Z)^{2}+\left[r_{3}\sin(\theta)\right]^{2}}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Quote

\series bold
Note on Literature Typo:

\series default
 The original manuscript published in 1988 contains a typographical error in the distance kernel expression,
 printing 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{3}\sin\theta$
\end_inset

 instead of 
\begin_inset Formula $r\sin\theta$
\end_inset

 in the second term of 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{1}$
\end_inset

.
 The derivation above confirms that 
\begin_inset Formula $r\sin\theta$
\end_inset

 is the physically correct radial component.
 Thus,
 its 
\series bold
identically equivalent
\series default
 to Wang–Haiting Equation under the coordinate shift 
\begin_inset Formula $Z=Z_{\text{WH}}=z-R$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Reduction of the Rod Image Field (
\begin_inset Formula $E_{I_{\text{rod}}}$
\end_inset

)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
From equation 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:Erod_theta3_theta4"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

,
 the axial electric field component produced by the hemispherical rod image charge distribution is derived as:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\vec{E}_{I_{\text{rod}}}(0,0,z)=-\frac{1}{2\epsilon_{r}\epsilon_{0}}\int^{\theta_{B}/2}_{0}\int^{L_{cs}}_{0}\frac{R\,r^{2}\rho(r)\sin(\theta)\cos(\theta_{4})}{r^{2}_{4}\,r_{2}}\,dr\,d\theta\,\hat{z}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{2}=\sqrt{(R+r\cos\theta)^{2}+(r\sin\theta)^{2}}$
\end_inset

 represents the radial distance from the coordinate origin 
\begin_inset Formula $\vec{O}$
\end_inset

 to the source element.
 The image charge position angle 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{3}$
\end_inset

,
 observer distance 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{4}$
\end_inset

,
 and directional projection angle 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{4}$
\end_inset

 are defined relative to the symmetry axis as:
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\cos(\theta_{3})=\frac{R+r\cos(\theta)}{r_{2}},\quad\sin(\theta_{3})=\frac{r\sin(\theta)}{r_{2}}\quad\cos(\theta_{4})=\frac{\left|z-r_{3}\cos(\theta_{3})\right|}{r_{4}}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
r_{4}=\sqrt{\left[z-r_{3}\cos(\theta_{3})\right]^{2}+\left[r_{3}\sin(\theta_{3})\right]^{2}}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

with 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{3}=R^{2}/r_{2}$
\end_inset

 defining the radial offset of the Kelvin image point.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Step 1:
 Origin Shift to the Wang–Haiting Coordinate Frame
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Applying the coordinate translation from our origin at the sphere center to the Wang–Haiting apex origin (
\begin_inset Formula $Z_{\text{WH}}=z-R\Rightarrow z=Z_{\text{WH}}+R$
\end_inset

):
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
z-r_{3}\cos(\theta_{3})=Z_{\text{WH}}+R-r_{3}\cos(\theta_{3})
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Substituting into the expression for 
\begin_inset Formula $\cos\theta_{4}$
\end_inset

 yields:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\cos(\theta_{4})=\frac{Z_{\text{WH}}+R-r_{3}\cos(\theta_{3})}{r_{4}}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Step 2:
 Direct Reduction to Wang–Haiting Formulation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The field equations match directly (using 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon=\epsilon_{r}\epsilon_{0}$
\end_inset

):
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
E_{I}=-\frac{1}{2\epsilon}\int^{\theta_{B}/2}_{0}\int^{L_{cs}}_{0}\frac{R\,r^{2}\rho(r)\sin(\theta)\cos(\theta_{4})}{r^{2}_{4}\,r_{2}}\,dr\,d\theta
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where the image distance kernel 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{4}$
\end_inset

 maps identically to:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
r_{4}=\sqrt{\left(Z_{\text{WH}}+R-r_{3}\cos(\theta_{3})\right)^{2}+\left(r_{3}\sin(\theta_{3})\right)^{2}}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Quote

\series bold
Note on Literature Typo:

\series default
 The original Wang and Haiting (1988) paper misprinted the second term inside the 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{4}$
\end_inset

 square root as 
\begin_inset Formula $(r_{3}\sin(\theta))^{2}$
\end_inset

 instead of 
\begin_inset Formula $(r_{3}\sin(\theta_{3}))^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 The derivation above confirms that 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{3}$
\end_inset

 is the correct angular argument.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This completes the proof that our coordinate-based implementation reduces directly to the classic Wang–Haiting formulation without requiring explicit evaluation of intermediate polar angle transformations during integration.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Newpage pagebreak
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Grounded Plane Image Field (
\begin_inset Formula $E_{I_{\text{plane}}}$
\end_inset

) and Boundary Approximations
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In long rod-plane gap discharge modeling (where gap distance 
\begin_inset Formula $d\gg R$
\end_inset

),
 the high-voltage ionization phenomena and streamer propagation characteristics occur primarily in the immediate vicinity of the rod tip.
 In the formulation of Wang and Haiting (1988),
 the contribution of the grounded plane image field 
\begin_inset Formula $\vec{E}_{I_{\text{plane}}}$
\end_inset

 is omitted (
\begin_inset Formula $\vec{E}_{I_{\text{plane}}}\approx0$
\end_inset

).
 Under this assumption,
 the total space-charge field along the gap axis reduces to:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\vec{E}_{\text{total, WH}}=\vec{E}_{ex}+\vec{E}_{sp}+\vec{E}_{I_{\text{rod}}}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In our complete model,
 the plane image field 
\begin_inset Formula $\vec{E}_{I_{\text{plane}}}$
\end_inset

 is explicitly retained to preserve exact boundary conditions across the entire inter-electrode gap length 
\begin_inset Formula $d$
\end_inset

.
 When evaluating field distributions near the rod electrode (
\begin_inset Formula $z\ll d$
\end_inset

),
 
\begin_inset Formula $\vec{E}_{I_{\text{plane}}}\to0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

