\begin_layout Section
Dynamic Kinematics and Relative Coordinate Tracking
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Float figure
placement H
alignment document
wide false
sideways false
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout
\align center
\begin_inset Graphics
	filename assets/space_charge_kinematics.pdf
	scale 60

\end_inset


\begin_inset Caption Standard

\begin_layout Plain Layout
Space Charge Kinematics
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To incorporate the spatial translation of the drifting space charge cloud along the primary axis of symmetry,
 the kinematics are integrated into the existing framework using a 
\series bold
continuous relative coordinate mapping
\series default
.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{\text{drift}}(t)$
\end_inset

 represent the time-dependent,
 absolute physical translation of the space charge cloud’s center of mass along the positive 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{z}$
\end_inset

 direction away from its initial coordinate.
 The actual position of any discrete charge element shifts dynamically over time according to:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
z_{\text{c}}(t)=z_{\text{c},0}+z_{\text{drift}}(t)\;\;\;\;,\;\;\;\;E_{\text{sp}}(z_{c,0})=0\implies z_{c,0}=E^{-1}_{\text{sp}}(0)
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Because the governing electrostatic field equations depend strictly on the relative displacement vector directed from the source charge coordinate to the target evaluation point (
\begin_inset Formula $\vec{P}_{observer}-\vec{P}_{charge,source}$
\end_inset

),
 the physical vertical tracking component transforms as follows:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align}
\text{Axial Displacement} & =z-z_{\text{c}}(t)\nonumber \\
 & =z-\left(z_{\text{c},0}+z_{\text{drift}}(t)\right)\nonumber \\
 & =\left(z-z_{\text{drift}}(t)\right)-z_{\text{c},0}
\end{align}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This algebraic grouping demonstrates that physically moving the space charge cloud forward by 
\begin_inset Formula $+z_{\text{drift}}(t)$
\end_inset

 is mathematically identical to keeping the cloud’s integration grid completely stationary and shifting the effective observation coordinate backward by 
\begin_inset Formula $-z_{\text{drift}}(t)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Consequently,
 the simulation engine preserves the static computational geometry of the mesh grid.
 To evaluate the resulting fields at an absolute location 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

,
 the framework simply passes a time-dependent effective observer coordinate into the static field integral algorithms:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
z_{\text{eff}}(t)=z-z_{\text{drift}}(t)
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This spatial substitution variable replaces the literal observer coordinate 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

 across all localized field equations,
 eliminating the computational overhead of dynamically rebuilding or translating multi-dimensional arrays during runtime.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Kinematic Time-Stepping and Discrete Integration
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The dynamic translation 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{\text{drift}}(t)$
\end_inset

 of the space charge cloud is governed by the time-varying total driving electric field evaluated at the moving center of mass 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{c}(t)$
\end_inset

.
 By framing the translation explicitly as a continuous function of time,
 the total accumulated drift distance at any instant 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 is resolved directly by integrating the cloud’s velocity:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
z_{\text{drift}}(t)=\int^{t}_{0}\underbrace{\mu^{+}\left[E_{\text{ex}}\left(\;z_{c}(\tau)\;\right)+E_{\text{I,rod}}\left(\;z_{c}(\tau)\;\right)+E_{\text{I,plane}}\left(\;z_{c}(\tau)\;\right)\right]}_{\left\Vert \vec{v}\right\Vert \;\left[m/s\right]:\;\text{The speed of the cloud's center of mass}}d\tau\;\;\left[m\right]
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where the baseline mobility coefficient for positive ions in atmospheric air is defined as:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mu^{+}=1.4\times10^{-4}\quad\left[\frac{\text{m}^{2}}{\text{V}\cdot\text{s}}\right]
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Decoupled Coordinate Mapping and Kinematic Reference Tracking
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To progress the system chronologically from time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $t+\Delta t$
\end_inset

,
 the continuous integration limits are partitioned according to the additive property of definite integrals:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
z_{\text{drift}}(t+\Delta t)=z_{\text{drift}}(t)+\int^{t+\Delta t}_{t}\mu^{+}\left[E_{\text{ex}}\left(\;z_{c}(\tau)\;\right)+E_{\text{I,rod}}\left(\;z_{c}(\tau)\;\right)+E_{\text{I,plane}}\left(\;z_{c}(\tau)\;\right)\right]d\tau
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The second term on the right-hand side represents the exact incremental spatial displacement 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{m}(t)$
\end_inset

 
\series bold
[1]
\series default
 over the step interval 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta t$
\end_inset

.
 Defining this step displacement yields:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
z_{m}(t)=\int^{t+\Delta t}_{t}\mu^{+}\left[E_{\text{ex}}\left(\;z_{c}(\tau)\;\right)+E_{\text{I,rod}}\left(\;z_{c}(\tau)\;\right)+E_{\text{I,plane}}\left(\;z_{c}(\tau)\;\right)\right]d\tau
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Substituting this definition back into the partitioned integral yields the clean time-dependent kinematic evolution law:
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
z_{\text{drift}}(t+\Delta t)=z_{\text{drift}}(t)+z_{m}(t)
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This mathematical formulation bridges continuous classical kinematics with discrete computational execution.
 The simulation engine simply advances a master time variable 
\begin_inset Formula $t\to t+\Delta t$
\end_inset

,
 which inherently maps the updated spatial boundaries for 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{\text{drift}}(t)$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{c}(t)$
\end_inset

,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{\text{eff}}(t)$
\end_inset

 without relying on software-specific accumulator variables.
\end_layout

